{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"records\": [\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S191-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"581bc27e51743920d701b2d42cc9fe98915ba0e330bb48f00782b39aa5a6bdb8\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 identifies one misplaced dot; A3 makes the marriage's standing depend on typography.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's twenty years with 'Socially'; A5 extends B4's children into inheritance.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 defend their shared marriage through duration and children.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B4 prompts A5 to extend the supposed defect into the next generation.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1's marriage certificate and B2's twenty years immediately establish spouses and stakes.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'Socially, certainly' efficiently preserves sincerity; A5's inheritance follows B4.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 88\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"This marriage certificate has the dot above the wrong letter.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We've been married twenty years.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Socially, certainly. Typographically, it's complicated.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We have three children.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then let's correct it before they inherit the error.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A treats one misplaced mark as the source of the marriage's legitimacy despite an established family life.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Ordinary proofreading would correct the certificate without casting doubt on the marriage itself.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 separates social marriage from typographic standing, and A5 treats the defect as something children could inherit.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative makes a spouse anxiously protect a family; alternative b makes a club official withhold release from membership.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained after rereading: both family facts change A's account of the same dot, and the original meets the internal threshold and spoken-turn gates.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 uses figurative inheritance, and A3 implies rather than explicitly denies official legitimacy.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S191-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"a16fbf028c86156fca660011290cf55c956c5819b31ec718b6e0fa79e6cbf1c9\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 and A3 hinge on seal orientation, but A5 substitutes an international-form requirement.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"B4 proposes turning the page; A5 gives no coherent reason this makes the submission international.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 wants departure acknowledged; B4 offers a practical fix to A's obstruction.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 adds another obstacle, but abandons the particular defect established in A1.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a club resignation and upside-down seal; B2 establishes distance.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'Travel doesn't rotate a seal' is earned; A5's international classification is arbitrary.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 61\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Your club resignation has the seal upside down. You're still our secretary.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I've moved abroad. Rotate the page.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"There. Now your resignation is valid.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Good. You can stop sending me the minutes.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'll rotate the previous eleven too. We may owe you a year's subscriptions.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A lets the orientation of one seal determine whether a resignation has official force.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"General bureaucratic obstruction can invent successive requirements instead of remaining committed to one validating detail.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A5 changes requirements; revised A3 accepts the rotated seal and A5 applies that same criterion retrospectively.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a fears an inherited family defect; this version lets a departing member's helpful fix expose the club's financial liability.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised within the club setting. Internal second pass: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 4, brevity 4 = 90. B4's request to stop the minutes makes A5 examine previous resignations and extend the correction to subscriptions. Five spoken turns, under 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 introduces eleven previous submissions; that is playable escalation but asks actors to absorb an unseen correspondence history.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S192-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"058d1ebedf34a070871d50a821d0c6ff89000a0bc22e485e5b1a6b01754ec3b6\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 converts an empty-chair coincidence into allocation doctrine; A5 converts kindness into entitlement.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2 supplies availability for A3; B4 supplies reservation for A5's revised policy.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 corrects A's assumption, then B4 voluntarily offers future companionship.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 upgrades accidental proximity into an ongoing reserved entitlement because of B4.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes lunch seating; B2 makes the initial encounter intelligible.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'Useful clarification' and A5's entitlement efficiently turn warmth into doctrine.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 90\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You sat beside me at lunch. Shall I record that as our friendship policy?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"It was the only empty chair.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"So friendship is allocated by availability. Useful clarification.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Tomorrow I'll save you a seat.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Excellent. We've moved from allocation to a reserved entitlement.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A interprets accidental seating and a personal offer as successive versions of an institutional friendship policy.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"A simple appetite for rules would prescribe behavior without deriving doctrine from B's actual choices.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 derives allocation from B2's empty chair; A5 updates the doctrine when B4 offers a reserved seat.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative institutionalizes a growing friendship through helpful offers; alternative b institutionalizes a personal act of repair.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained after rereading because B's kindness changes the policy and advances the relationship without requiring an argument.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A1 announces the policy vocabulary immediately, leaving little gradual discovery of A's interpretive habit.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S192-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"464fb6b1bc6225c835025944f6910ac819ffa7899542c63e6dbc08db9a8ad3c1\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 makes apology and tea a procedure; A3 derives a damage rule from B2.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's broken cup; A5 incorporates B4's remorse as documentation.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 explains the accident and B4 asserts feeling, but neither strongly changes available choices.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 adds a document metaphor without showing a larger consequence of A3's doctrine.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1's tea and B2's knocked-over cup establish a domestic repair.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 compresses the mistaken causal rule; A5's optional remorse follows B4's feeling.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 80\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You apologised before making tea. Is that our conflict-resolution procedure now?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I knocked over your cup.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"So material damage triggers refreshments.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Yesterday I made tea because you looked sad. Nothing broke.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then sadness qualifies as damage. I'll amend the procedure before either of us has to smash anything.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A connects personal acts of care into a general institutional rule and incorporates exceptions by amending its categories.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Bureaucratic vocabulary alone decorates a conversation without making personal evidence generate a larger system.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised B4 supplies a second tea-making occasion; A5 expands the damage category to reconcile both occasions.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a turns a new friendship into an entitlement; this version makes a household care policy broad enough to cover emotional distress.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised in the existing tea setting. Internal second pass: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 4, brevity 4 = 90. B4's counterexample causes A5's wider doctrine and practical commitment. Five spoken turns, under 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5's concern about smashing things is an exaggerated implication, though it follows the newly established damage rule.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S193-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"ec5554903251639c749aa708da47c26899401b0b4e1a2e02a21cc43c80e27cba\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 claims victory authorizes a favorable theme; A3 claims authority over fairness.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's fairness objection, but A5 merely repeats B4's half-point as a committee name.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 objects generally; B4 supplies a margin but no concrete alternative or consequence.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 claims another chairmanship without showing what the half-point committee controls.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1's bake-off and familiar cakes are playable; A5's committee remit is unclear.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's leadership slogan is concise, but A5's committee feels assembled from B4's noun.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 68\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I won the bake-off, so next year's theme is cakes I already make.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You beat my lemon cake by half a point.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then lemon cakes clearly get a fair chance under my leadership.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Fine. I'll enter that same cake again.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Excellent. Returning challengers must improve by a full point. The champion sets the standard.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A treats winning as authority to define the next competition and the fairness of rules that protect the winner.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Ordinary competitive cheating seeks an advantage without treating previous success as a mandate to govern.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 invokes the win as authority, and revised A5 explicitly makes the champion the source of the next standard.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative changes competitive eligibility against a close rival; alternative b extends a family victory into control over its reward.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised within the bake-off setting. Internal second pass: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 4, brevity 4 = 90. B4's decision to reenter the near-winning cake causes A5 to impose a larger improvement requirement. Five spoken turns, under 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The rule is transparently self-serving; A needs sincere confidence in the champion's authority rather than a conspiratorial delivery.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S193-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"86c9c543785f90713428ef82aa1a29de620af994bdd4b7065e6ce5716304d610\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 converts bowling victory into dinner authority; A5 explicitly claims the right to define the next event.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 reinterprets B2's trophy, and A5 uses B4's exclusion of dinner as an opening for new terms.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 both repeat the original agreement rather than adding a materially different pressure.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 restates winner authority without naming a new event or increasing the practical demand.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes family bowling and dinner; B2 specifies the agreed trophy.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'judgement under pressure' is apt, but A5 ends in an abstract statement of the card.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 78\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"As winner of family bowling, I choose where we eat.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We only agreed you'd get a trophy.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"A trophy recognises judgement under pressure. Mine says pizza.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Your brother can't eat cheese.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then the next event is finding pizza he can eat. I'll award myself the biggest slice for organising it.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A uses a prior success as a license to expand the reward and set the next family challenge.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Generic bossiness demands dinner control without deriving that authority from a particular win.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 anchors authority in bowling; A3 interprets the trophy as judgment credentials; A5 adds a winner-designed event and reward.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a protects a champion against a close rival; this version accommodates a family constraint while awarding A more authority and pizza.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised within family bowling and dinner. Internal second pass: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 4, brevity 4 = 90. B4's dietary constraint causes A5's new search event and self-awarded reward. Five spoken turns, under 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The cheese constraint is deliberately brief; the scene leaves the actual meal and brother's response open.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S194-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"7ace755fc6736743fa5062db6c4d5c5d919006bf832847bdd1bacf832c11343d\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 dismisses defeat and stakes reputation on a rematch; A5's tea contest loses that explicit standing anxiety.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's thirty years; A5 turns B4's wish for tea into a replacement contest.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 offers long acquaintance as reassurance; B4 wants ordinary companionship over tea.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 changes the competition to tea but does not raise what its result means for A's standing.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a lost chess game; B2 and B4 establish a longstanding social ritual.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'Best tea wins' follows B4 cleanly, although it primarily demonstrates competitiveness.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 77\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"That lost chess game was practice. This rematch will establish my reputation.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We've played here for thirty years.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Exactly. I won't have thirty years remembered for one dropped rook.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I just came for tea.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then I'll make it. One excellent pot and you'll remember why you keep coming back.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A projects control while treating a loss as a threat to longstanding importance and making the next result responsible for restoring it.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Simple competitiveness would turn tea into another contest without fearing the loss of B's regard.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 fears thirty years being reduced to a dropped rook; A5 makes tea prove why B should keep visiting.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative shifts threatened standing into a friendship ritual; alternative b removes karaoke equipment and raises the exposure of A's performance.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised within the chess-and-tea setting. Internal second pass: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 4, brevity 4 = 90. B4's wish for tea makes the next pot responsible for validating the whole friendship. Five spoken turns, under 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A3 exposes anxiety more directly than the original; confident delivery must preserve the instruction's outward assurance.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S194-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"e1c43a7ede3169e19c33783ab96e8dd6287564adf40b63e5eb0e84661a7f4f83\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 stakes continued ability on the next song after a bad score; A3 insists on flawless proof despite reassurance.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's absence of doubt as something to preserve; A5 turns B4's unplugging into an unmediated test.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 reassures A, while B4 removes the expected scoring apparatus and performance route.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 makes the exposed voice itself the test once B4 removes the machine.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1's karaoke score and B4's unplugged machine provide immediate playable circumstances.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'keep it that way' turns kindness into pressure; A5 makes the obstacle an opportunity.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 90\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Ignore my terrible karaoke score. The next song determines whether I still have it.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Nobody doubted you.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Excellent. One flawless song will keep it that way.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The machine's been unplugged.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then we finally have a chance to judge the voice itself.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A reframes each setback as a more important opportunity to prove continued worth while sounding confidently in control.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Optimistic persistence would welcome another song without making it determine whether A still has ability or standing.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 says the next song determines whether A 'still' has it, and A3 makes perfection necessary to prevent doubt.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a seeks renewed value in a long friendship; this version intensifies a public performance test when technology disappears.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained after rereading: reassurance increases A's self-imposed obligation, and unplugging creates a more exposed version of the same proof.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 leaves the audience and exact consequence implicit; the threatened standing depends on carrying A1's stakes forward.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S195-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"c1c32619d0984537b76388c0578d5119ae7eddb4cc2041688bc4652dd3cd998d\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 demands unstretched measurement and A3 rejects visual judgment; A5 asserts expertise without correcting the method.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B4 identifies a knitted table cover, but A5 leaves unclear how knowing its stretch validates a calibrated table.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 is a rival claimant, and B4 challenges A's measuring apparatus through A's own handiwork.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 defends A's knowledge but adds no precision or consequence to the measurement.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes scarf length clearly; A3's calibrated table and B4's cover make the apparatus hard to picture.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 uses B4's knitting fact, but the stretch explanation needs an unstated measurement procedure.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 69\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You can't claim longest scarf until we've measured without stretching it.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"It's obviously longer than yours.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then you won't mind laying both flat against this tape.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Yours only reaches because of the tassels.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Quite right. Separate fringe measurements. I may have to surrender overall length and retain decorative reach.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A makes precise measurement and category distinctions a prerequisite for recognizing either competitor's success.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Sore-loser obstruction disputes results to preserve victory without showing an actual commitment to measurement craft.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A5 accepts B4's tassel objection and allows a loss in overall length while distinguishing decorative reach.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative tests the scorer's own entry against a rival; alternative b adapts certification when a cake's evidence has vanished.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised within the scarf contest, replacing the unclear table with a tape. Internal second pass: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 92. B4's fringe objection causes A5's separate measurements and possible concession. Five spoken turns, under 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"Decorative reach is A's proposed category, so performance should emphasize exact classification rather than a transparent consolation prize.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S195-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"121ca912d1122b130a45b38b8556eaedf8841503b7d46c4dffb57cc1da601697\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 asks for a scoring scale, A3 rejects inconsistent judging, and A5 restricts what can be certified.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 challenges B2's love as a measure; A5 replaces presentation after B4 says the cake was eaten.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 defends the cake through approval; B4 makes A's requested visual assessment unavailable.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 narrows the entire certifiable result to consumption because B4 removes the evidence.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a scored cake; B4 supplies the concrete complication of its disappearance.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's consistency objection is concise; A5's consumption category grows directly from B4.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 90\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"The cake got nine out of ten for taste. What's the denominator for presentation?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Everyone loved it.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Love isn't scored consistently between judges.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"They've eaten the evidence.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then 'most thoroughly consumed' is the only category I can certify.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A insists that a success claim match a defensible scoring standard and the evidence still available.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Generic pedantry corrects terminology without regulating how success may be claimed.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 rejects love because judges cannot score it consistently; A5 explicitly limits certification to an observable outcome.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a involves a rival's challenge to A's own scarf; this version handles disappearing evidence without needing A to compete.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained after rereading because B's report of consumption forces a specific change in what A permits the result to mean.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The superlative 'most' implies a comparison not supplied in the scene; the category remains an illustrative proposal.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S196-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"780cab4481a7b98c1caa5718a6e1ccd3d51aec41c9825c6916cfba29a82ed07d\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 offers balanced accounts before an invitation challenge; A5 offers another competence display after rejection.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 says votes have already been counted after B2 says a vote is needed, without establishing that one occurred.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 defend the joining procedure, but B4 supplies no new practical obstacle.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 expands into process design, though the claim that joining loses money is unsupported.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1's committee accounts and B2's membership vote establish a clear participation dispute.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 efficiently exposes the need to earn admission; A5's money claim feels attached for the closing line.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 74\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Before you ask who invited me to the committee, I've balanced its accounts.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You still need a membership vote.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Could you circulate the accounts with my application? Especially the savings.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We can't vote tonight. Nobody's taken the minutes.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I have. Alphabetised, with action points. Shall I read the section where you need a treasurer?\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A responds to uncertain membership by making completed, useful work increasingly visible as evidence of deserving a place.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"A power grab seeks committee control; this behavior uses competence to establish a right to participate.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 attaches work to a membership application, and A5 answers a procedural delay with another completed service.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative meets a formal admission barrier with documentary competence; alternative b turns a welcoming invitation into self-imposed performance duties.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised within the c